#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b20fab-b12e-503c-9d76-9e32b70d28b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121178} {:answer/id #uuid "b223a4c9-a07c-5de9-b93b-5bec37165c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121179} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe5c7ec-db6c-506a-b5df-b8b8ee38049f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121180} {:answer/id #uuid "250f73d2-c369-5bfe-9b0b-44ccb047104f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121181} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5d48c7-e814-5195-8684-7e78197a6881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffe5c7ec-db6c-506a-b5df-b8b8ee38049f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121180} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc4ac47-7151-579b-8619-a9ae3ec96407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cac3744a-0cdf-56d6-88ab-9bba2eefa254" :question/math-academy-id "q-111192" :question/problem "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many days, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the bicycles will be at the station. Therefore, the total number of bikes at the station after many days will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 720=120.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6987f7-d047-5725-a2bd-3e2bca3a52e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355406} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d47aa1-227e-5429-b9e4-c00b74675cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355407} {:answer/id #uuid "913ebd2f-d0c1-519f-8eef-cdbfebdecc27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355408} {:answer/id #uuid "21c7d5ab-3661-5ad3-918b-38d90e9867ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355409} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a88753-13ce-5f92-9515-abffd87ad64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c7d5ab-3661-5ad3-918b-38d90e9867ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355409} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42354f6-7767-55fd-8119-59e508a5c59c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aad6a8d-de7b-580b-9006-f42447edc883" :question/math-academy-id "q-135307" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5e7659b-e353-5d73-ad99-21529515e9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355413} {:answer/id #uuid "3b44d241-54e9-5e25-b6ec-4c9cedff8b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355414} {:answer/id #uuid "6a93fa76-d8ee-5879-87ac-acfc4c6a0bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355415} {:answer/id #uuid "9369c480-8fe5-5cda-bcc7-7c040fcc6333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355416} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2adb16-07f5-52c5-8cd5-37172df02d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a93fa76-d8ee-5879-87ac-acfc4c6a0bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355415} :answer-field/id #uuid "af3c6266-c133-5f31-abbb-11c4e1ac52ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f4f0622-d148-5c14-a65a-6f7875352272" :question/math-academy-id "q-135324" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.5{x}_{1}+0.9{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{14}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{14} \\\\ \\frac{5}{14}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{9}{14}-\\frac{5}{14}=\\frac{2}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4555c419-2275-5785-af39-77f0ce77a00b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355420} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f3f075-422d-52dd-a931-06e5cd04a571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355421} {:answer/id #uuid "304c8ee3-241e-532e-97f7-674d10e8276c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355422} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffc62f1-f3ea-5d71-96db-7faeabc963b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355423} {:answer/id #uuid "34b8d71a-3ccc-5625-9be7-b403cef61a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "304c8ee3-241e-532e-97f7-674d10e8276c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355422} :answer-field/id #uuid "19bc8d3c-416d-5bec-a4ac-1463ee5136d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0326e9e0-b765-518b-b15a-f208aa3a5357" :question/math-academy-id "q-135345" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.35\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.5 & 0.35 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.35 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 3.5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 3.5 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-8{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -21 & 31.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21 & -31.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -21 & 31.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{31.5}{21}{x}_{3}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})-3.5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0394806f-855f-5440-a558-98f6df461896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355427} {:answer/id #uuid "f61979a4-aea0-5888-a38e-e663c6afc97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355428} {:answer/id #uuid "5c68f116-da75-5634-9a87-8807080872cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355429} {:answer/id #uuid "459eb385-0802-5de3-b210-c2c50c923b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355430} {:answer/id #uuid "191e6530-ec90-5f1b-a83b-bfa789cf42e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "191e6530-ec90-5f1b-a83b-bfa789cf42e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355431} :answer-field/id #uuid "3509a5f6-7181-5f9e-bf07-c0e65d9dbc91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cc49333-cb3b-5c48-861c-eaf8b83eb368" :question/math-academy-id "q-135407" :question/problem "A town planning department wants to know how its citizens' preferences for traveling to work are changing. So they conducted a survey, and the results are shown below.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Subway** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| **Subway** | $0.5$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nThey found that $50%$ of those surveyed that currently drive to work are planning to switch to the subway next year, while $60%$ of the people surveyed that currently use the subway are planning to switch to their cars next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $50\\,000$ that travel to work by car or subway, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use their cars to drive to work? Round your answer to the nearest hundreds." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{11}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{11} \\\\ \\frac{5}{11}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{6}{11}$ of the $50\\,000$ people surveyed would use their cars. Therefore, the total number of people who will eventually use their cars is\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{11}\\cdot 50\\,000≈27\\,300.\n$$"}]}
